      "id": "tension_ptx",
      "title": "Tension pneumothorax — the burglar who can't leave",
      "image": "media/scene.png",
      "source": "Cardiothoracic Surgery 2e, Ch17 p823",
      "story": "Building = rib cage (floors = intercostal spaces, roof rail = clavicle). A car crashes (rapid deceleration) and snaps a rib-beam that pierces the lung balloon; a man holding his breath with a zipped mouth pops tiny balloons (closed glottis → alveolar rupture). Air fills the room: the lung gasps and clutches its chest; knocking echoes like a drum (hyper-resonance); the stethoscope hears only a cricket (absent breath sounds). Tension: walls turn to bubble wrap (subcutaneous emphysema), the central wall bows (mediastinal shift), the heart is squeezed and the gauge swings (hemodynamic instability). The firefighter stabs a big needle from the 2nd-floor balcony at the middle of the clavicle rail; the plumber fits a permanent pipe in the side wall between floors 4 and 5, pointing up and forward. The chainsaw stays in its dusty case: thoracotomy rarely indicated.",
